WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:06.040
Movies aren’t always designed to be easy
to understand.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.990
Sometimes, it’s your own fault for not paying
more attention.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:12.830
But there are also times when the entire audience
just doesn’t get it.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:16.700
Here are a few scenes that needed a little
bit of extra explaining...

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:17.700
Donnie Darko

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:25.360
Fact: nobody understood Donnie Darko the first
time around—especially the insane ending.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.740
It’s the world.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.300
Because… well, alternate reality time travel
stuff.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.210
It’s all explained in the director’s cut,
but some viewers believe that Donnie didn’t

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:39.739
actually have to die to save the world, and
that sending the jet engine through time was

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:40.739
enough.

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:43.470
Death isn’t inevitable, but Donnie knows
what’s going to happen.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:47.309
He lets himself die because he feels at peace
by the time all of his alternate universe

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:49.520
time travel shenanigans are over.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.320
One-ness with the universe or not, that bunny
was still pretty freaky.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.160
Star Trek

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.780
During the climax of J.J. Abrams' Star Trek,
Spock ignites a black hole inside the villain's’

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.760
ship, and this scene plays out.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.740
“The ship is compromised, the ship is too
close to the singularity to survive without

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.890
assistance which we are willing to provide.”

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:12.440
“Captain, what are you doing?”

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.800
“Show them compassion maybe the only way
to earn peace with the Romulans.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.020
It’s logic Spock.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.380
THought you would like that.”

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.800
“No, not really.”

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.580
Confused viewers had to wonder: is Spock giving
in to human emotions?

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:29.360
Those bad guys had previously destroyed Spock's
homeworld and killed his family, after all.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.630
But Spock’s behavior is actually 100% Vulcan.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.470
Early in the movie, young Spock learns the
ethical concept of "supererogation," or as

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:37.520
they define it:

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.250
“When is it morally praiseworthy but not
morally obligatory.”

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:45.440
Most viewers didn't remember the earlier Vulcan
school scene… but that black hole business?

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.330
Pure Vulcan ethics.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:48.330
“Fascinating”

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:49.560
Total Recall

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.560
Arnold Schwarzenegger plays a man named Douglas
Quaid who visits a company to have artificial

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:58.420
memories of a secret agent adventure implanted
in his brain, but something goes wrong.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:02.610
Turns out that Quaid is really a sleeper agent,
and the fake memory process starts to reactivate

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:03.879
his real memories.

00:02:03.879 --> 00:02:06.220
Or is that just part of the imaginary adventure?

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.920
“If I am not me,who the hell am I?”

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:12.200
Director Paul Verhoeven put the true answer
in the movie.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.790
During the final shot, Quaid stands on the
mountains of Mars, and there's a bright flash

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:16.790
from the sun.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:19.980
So, the initial procedure actually did go
terribly wrong.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:20.980
Happy ending?

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:21.980
Nope.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.260
It’s a doctor cutting into a man's wrecked
brain.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:25.480
“I have a headache.”

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.230
“It might be a tumor?”

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.120
“It’s not a tumor.”

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.000
American Psycho

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.780
Patrick Bateman is a man who spends his nights
committing increasingly vile acts of violence.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.520
“Hey Paul, AHHHHH”

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.030
He emotionlessly narrates his experiences,
making statements like, well…

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.130
“I simply am not there”

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:48.290
Eventually, he calls his lawyer and leaves
a message confessing to his crimes, but when

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:51.660
he confronts his lawyer in person, he doesn't
even recognize Bateman.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.290
Fans have debated whether or not Bateman’s
murders are actually real.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:59.100
But according to director Mary Harmon, the
point of the lawyer scene isn't to cast doubt

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.490
on Bateman's murders, but to emphasize that
it doesn't matter.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.430
Whatever Bateman is, he's normal.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:07.990
Wall Street, and the whole world, is full
of self-involved psychopaths.

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:10.420
How’s that for bleak?

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:12.220
Lost in Translation

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.010
An aging movie star named Bob meets Charlotte,
a married woman, and the two of them connect

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:17.010
deeply.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.670
Lost in Translation ends with the them about
to part ways forever, when Bob runs to find

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:21.670
Charlotte.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:24.150
They hug, and he whispers something in her
ear before leaving.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.819
Most assumed that this was a romantic ending,
but it was meant to be heartbreaking.

00:03:27.819 --> 00:03:31.850
In the original script, Bob tells Charlotte
to stay with her husband, unable to tell her

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:32.870
that he loves her.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.900
Honestly, we preferred the mystery.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.220
Thanks for watching!

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:39.209
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:43.220
And leave us a comment to let us know which
movie endings you’d like to see explained...

